I have everything I need. Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-Smell Academic Catalog** audit for your integration test suite.

---

## Summary Dashboard

```
┌──────────┬─────────────┬────────────────┐
│ Severity │ Smell Count │ Affected Tests │
├──────────┼─────────────┼────────────────┤
│ High     │ 3           │ 3              │
│ Medium   │ 1           │ 1              │
│ Low      │ 0           │ 0              │
│ Total    │ 4           │ 4              │
└──────────┴─────────────┴────────────────┘
```

4 of 8 test methods (50%) carry at least one smell. The other 4 are clean.

---

## Findings by Severity

### ❶ Sleepy Test — HIGH

**Affected:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);                                      // ← smell
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Why it matters:** A hard-coded 3-second sleep makes the suite slower and non-deterministic. On a loaded CI machine, 3 s may not be enough; on a fast machine, it's wasted time. Even for integration tests, `Thread.Sleep` is flagged because a polling/retry wait is strictly better.

**Integration calibration:** Severity remains **High**. The academic catalog (Camara et al., SAST 2021) correlates `Sleepy Test` with flakiness regardless of test scope.

**Fix — poll with a timeout instead:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll up to 5 s, fail fast when ready
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(5);
    while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
    {
        Assert.IsTrue(DateTime.UtcNow < deadline,
            "Notification was not sent within the timeout.");
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures and a 3 s floor on suite duration that multiplies as tests grow.

---

### ❷ Conditional Test Logic — HIGH

**Affected:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101-109)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)          // ← smell
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Why it matters:** One branch is never exercised per run. If the production code changes which type is returned, the test silently starts validating a different branch — hiding regressions.

**Integration calibration:** NOT downgraded. Conditional Test Logic is problematic at every scope — control flow in tests creates hidden gaps regardless of whether it's a unit or integration test.

**Fix — split into two deterministic tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<User>(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    var user = new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m);
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(((PremiumUser)loaded).DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Silent test gap — one code path is never validated.

---

### ❸ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — HIGH

**Affected:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113-119)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion
}
```

**Why it matters:** This test passes even if `Insert` silently discards every record. MSTest marks it green as long as no exception is thrown — it provides false confidence.

**Note:** The method name `*_RunsWithoutErrors` suggests a *does-not-throw* intent, which is a legitimate (if weak) implicit assertion. Still flagged because the behavior can be made explicit and the test can verify the *outcome*, not just the absence of exceptions.

**Fix — assert the postcondition:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — the test provides coverage metrics without actually verifying anything.

---

### ❹ Assertion Roulette — MEDIUM

**Affected:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35-39)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);                          // which failed?
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);             // which failed?
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email); // which failed?
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);                      // which failed?
```

**Why it matters:** 4 assertions with no message parameters. When a CI build fails, the stack trace points to a line number but the test-results XML summary just says "test failed." In a dashboard or email notification, you cannot tell *which property* was wrong without opening the code. (Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020 — Assertion Roulette is one of the two most prevalent smells in the wild.)

**Integration calibration:** Severity stays **Medium**. Integration tests often have multi-property verification, but message parameters cost nearly nothing to add.

**Fix — add assertion messages:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower triage — developers must open source code to understand CI failures.

---

## Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The remaining **4 tests are clean** and demonstrate good integration-test design:

| Test Method | Why it's good |
|---|---|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, 2 focused assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single assertion verifying the postcondition (null) |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count assertion (3) is self-documenting after inserting 3 items — not a Magic Number smell |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Properly `await`s `Task.WhenAll`, verifies count matches range — clean async pattern |

The `[TestInitialize]` / `[TestCleanup]` setup is also well-structured: both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by **all** 8 tests, so there is **no General Fixture** smell.

